# The repository had no .gitignore at all. The entries below are grouped by what goes
# wrong when the file is missing, because the first group is a security control and the
# rest are hygiene.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS - the reason this file is a security control rather than a convenience
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env files carry the values .env.example describes with placeholders: DATABASE_URL with
# its password, SECRET_KEY, and the Firebase configuration. Nothing prevented `git add .`
# from committing one, and a secret that reaches history stays reachable in history after
# it is deleted from the working tree - the remedy is rotating the credential, not another
# commit. .env.example is the documented template and is deliberately tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Service-account keys and certificates. This application is designed never to need one -
# it authenticates through Workload Identity and Application Default Credentials, and
# infrastructure/terraform issues no key for either service account - so any file matching
# these belongs to a local experiment and must not be published.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
service-account*.json
gcp-credentials*.json

# Terraform state records every provisioned resource and, for some resource types, the
# attribute values used to create them. It is also where a Cloud SQL password would land
# if the database user were ever managed here, which is precisely why an operator
# provisions that login by hand instead.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
crash.log

# Firebase CLI local state, which can hold a login token.
.firebase/
firebase-debug.log
.runtimeconfig.json

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD OUTPUT AND DEPENDENCIES
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frontend/package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It is absent today, which is a
# recorded residual - .github/workflows/ci.yml runs `npm ci` and `npm audit`, and both
# need it - so it must be committable the moment it is generated correctly.
node_modules/
frontend/build/
frontend/.eslintcache
dist/
build/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
venv/
.venv/
env/
.Python

# Test and coverage artefacts
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
collection.txt
frontend-state.txt

# Verification evidence: screenshots and screen recordings written here by browser-driven
# validation. Kept out of the repository because it is evidence for a report rather than
# source, and because it is binary and grows with every run - a few runs of it would dwarf
# the codebase. Ignored rather than deleted so a report can still cite the paths.
blitzy/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL AND EDITOR NOISE
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
*.log

# Screenshots, screen recordings and other evidence written into the working tree by automated
# review tooling. They are records of a verification run rather than project content, they are
# large binaries, and committing them would put a transient artefact into history for good. The
# entries above already exclude the same class of thing for the test tooling (collection.txt,
# frontend-state.txt), so this follows that precedent rather than setting a new one.
blitzy/
